#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8823679c-4206-593e-9924-bba1b808b386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288397} {:answer/id #uuid "9445efe6-ebcf-5fed-8a56-5ce164ddda33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288398} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce72c8-fabe-53b4-9353-dea6391c44b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399} {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} {:answer/id #uuid "32999de3-d390-5e7b-8e44-6029adc7ac8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bd091d-91d9-5843-968d-693b832cddd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efd48b95-db5f-52c0-99e2-d4c8610ebe36" :question/math-academy-id "q-106465" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$"}]}
